\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The response of a tilted site}
\date{2026-11-19}

\p{Let a finite group act isometrically on a real inner product space
and let #{\nu} be uniform probability on the scalar orbit
#{\mathcal O}. Its points have norm #{a_0}, and its barycenter is
#{o_{\rm av}=\int o\,\extd\nu(o)}. Use a nonnegative
\vocabk{orbit gauge–Higgs weight}{fdip-010A}. At a selected site
#{x} take the additional factor
#{t_\eta(o)=\ue^{\eta\langle u,o\rangle}}, where #{u} is a fixed
field vector and #{\eta\in\mathbb R}. Allow arbitrary additional
nonnegative site factors at the other sites and assume their joint
partition with this tilt is positive.}

\p{The exact probability of the scalar at #{x} has density
#{\frac{t_\eta}{\int t_\eta\,\extd\nu}} relative to #{\nu}. For every
such finite graph and all its invariant couplings,
##{\bigl\|\langle\phi_x\rangle_\eta-o_{\rm av}\bigr\|
   \le a_0\bigl(\ue^{2|\eta|\|u\|a_0}-1\bigr).}
Thus as #{\eta\to0} the mean converges to the barycenter uniformly
in the graph and these couplings. When the orbit is centered,
#{o_{\rm av}=0}, the mean tends to zero and its norm also has the
elementary bound #{a_0}.}

\proof{
\p{By \ref{fdip-010Q}, the probability mass at #{o} is
#{t_\eta(o)/\sum_{o'}t_\eta(o')}. Dividing this mass by the
uniform mass #{1/|\mathcal O|} gives the stated density.
Cauchy–Schwarz bounds each factor and hence its orbit average between
#{\ue^{-|\eta|\|u\|a_0}} and #{\ue^{|\eta|\|u\|a_0}}. Their ratio lies
between #{\ue^{-2|\eta|\|u\|a_0}} and #{\ue^{2|\eta|\|u\|a_0}}, so its
distance from one is at most #{\ue^{2|\eta|\|u\|a_0}-1}. The
displacement of the mean is the
integral of #{o} times that density minus the integral of #{o}.
The triangle inequality and #{\|o\|=a_0} give the estimate.
A probability average of vectors of norm #{a_0} itself has norm
at most #{a_0}.}
}

\p{For the reflected two-point orbit in \ref{fdip-00ZZ} the
barycenter is a nonzero vector on the fixed axis. The zero-source site
mean of its gauge model equals this barycenter.}
